<h2 id="distinctions">Distinctions that matter</h2>

<dl>
  <dt>A full rate is not what everyone gets</dt>
  <dd>The mean new State Pension in payment in March 2026 was {{ m.f("nsp-mean-weekly") }} a week, at the rates then in force. <a href="/state-pension/#what-people-receive">What people actually receive</a>.</dd>
  <dt>The State Pension is not the same as Pension Credit</dt>
  <dd>Pension Credit is a separate, means-tested benefit. It has to be claimed, and many people who could get it do not. <a href="/state-pension/#extra-help">Pension Credit and other help</a>.</dd>
  <dt>A lower amount than under another rule is not a cut in pounds</dt>
  <dd>Under most alternatives to the triple lock the pension still rises each year. It rises by less. <a href="/reform-options/compare/">Compare the rules</a>.</dd>
  <dt>What the triple lock has cost is not what changing it would save</dt>
  <dd>Past increases are already in today's rates. A change affects future increases only. <a href="/reform-options/costs/#cost-and-saving">Costs and savings</a>.</dd>
  <dt>A research budget is not a measure of poverty, and neither is what people spend</dt>
  <dd>They answer different questions. <a href="/living-on-a-pension/#three-questions">Three questions about living costs</a>.</dd>
  <dt>State Pension rules are kept the same across the UK. Social care policy is set by each nation</dt>
  <dd>A care proposal for England does not cover people in Scotland, Wales or Northern Ireland. <a href="/reform-options/social-care/#care-scope">Who each policy covers</a>.</dd>
</dl>

